1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:10,040
This project takes a cats-versus-dogs model from image data to a working, monitored API.

2
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:24,369
The same project code connects data preparation, training, testing, deployment and monitoring.

3
00:00:25,569 --> 00:00:46,432
The full dataset stays versioned with DVC. I use a balanced, repeatable subset for the baseline run.

4
00:00:47,632 --> 00:01:11,634
Here is the completed MLflow run, with the settings and results from training on Kaggle.

5
00:01:12,834 --> 00:01:25,932
On the held-out test set, the baseline reached 80.05 percent accuracy and 79.99 percent macro F1.

6
00:01:27,132 --> 00:01:52,228
The repository keeps the code, configuration, data lists, tests and deployment files in one place.

7
00:01:53,428 --> 00:02:12,696
A push to main runs the tests, checks the saved model, builds Docker and publishes the image.

8
00:02:13,896 --> 00:02:28,540
This is the Docker image produced by the successful workflow and stored in GitHub's registry.

9
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:48,682
The Windows runner pulled that image, updated the service and checked a real prediction.

10
00:02:49,882 --> 00:03:08,619
The deployed API provides a health check, image prediction and simple request metrics.

11
00:03:09,819 --> 00:03:20,728
This known cat image is classified correctly, with probabilities for both classes.

12
00:03:21,928 --> 00:03:32,838
The same deployed service also returns the expected result for this known dog image.

13
00:03:34,038 --> 00:03:44,057
If the upload is not a valid image, the API returns a clear error instead of trying to predict.

14
00:03:45,257 --> 00:04:01,494
The service records how many predictions were made, any errors, and the average response time.

15
00:04:02,694 --> 00:04:16,588
Finally, I checked the live API with 20 labelled images: 10 cats and 10 dogs.

16
00:04:17,788 --> 00:04:23,988
That completes the full assignment path, from versioned data to a deployed and checked model.
